 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Shader compilation is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of operations in the stack, as it requires a single pass to build the AST and a recursive pass to generate GLSL [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the stack and the AST tree [S1].
-- **Performance:** Caching prevents recompilation on every frame, making runtime evaluation efficient. The bottleneck is the initial compilation [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Intuitive interface for building complex scenes; lazy evaluation ensures efficiency [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Limited to the primitives and operations explicitly implemented in the editor; debugging generated GLSL can be difficult due to the implicit stack-to-tree conversion [S1].
+Caching via `#invalidate` prevents recompilation on every frame, making runtime evaluation efficient [S1]. The Config class uses lazy evaluation and caching to avoid unnecessary shader recompilation [S1]. The interface is described as intuitive once the input model is understood [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The Stack-Based SDF Editor was developed as part of a final project for COS 426, focusing on raymarching with Signed Distance Fields [S1]. It was designed to demonstrate how SDFs can be built up from primitives and operations using a stack-based interface similar to RPN notation [S1].
